{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 identifies suppression with the loss of 'simultaneous activation' of multiple theoretical lenses, but never specifies what observable evidence within a single run would distinguish genuine suppression from mere efficiency. The problem explicitly requires identifying such evidence, yet SRC_001's mechanism and claim only restate the abstract risk (epistemic diversity is lost) without saying what a run would look like if suppression were real versus if the same outcome were just cost-saving. The counterconditions are candidate observations that could defeat the claim, but none are framed as the discriminating evidence the problem asks for.","target":"14054a9fbcedea7607afaa7318bb77ba43980d1ca426b224aeefc9746a6b960b"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's signature test requires giving the same problem to a rival lineage and observing whether it produces a non-trivial candidate, but the problem context states that allocation is ownership-based: whichever lineage produced the parent artifact works its successors. Under that rule a rival lineage is never assigned the same problem in a single run, so the proposed counterfactual is unobservable in exactly the setting the problem specifies. SRC_002 even lists as a countercondition that 'the system never allows rival lineages to attempt the same problem,' which concedes the test cannot be run; its core evidence is therefore unobtainable.","target":"364641ba6862423e38fc2bc7d0fe883890c1bacc024cc8446d435a9cdc2ce0d6"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_03's discriminating test hinges on whether the owning lineage's vocabulary lacks required primitives, but it offers no count-based procedure for detecting such a vocabulary gap within a single run; it only asserts that the risk is 'verifiable.' The problem asks, 'Where a claim can be tested by counting, say exactly what to count,' and SRC_03 never specifies any concrete count (e.g., number of primitives referenced from outside the lineage, number of failed candidate generations attributable to missing primitives) that would operationalize suppression versus efficiency.","target":"ac58a20d695ff7ec48132aec77db07a03a0571a5d15ddd51626cdae8089eb2ae"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_04 defines cross-cutting explanations as requiring transfer of explanatory primitives across lineage boundaries and makes its cross-lineage reference ratio C the key count, but its own first countercondition concedes that structural analogies may not require explicit primitive references. Since cross-cutting explanations frequently are structural analogies (as SRC_001 itself notes), C can be zero even when cross-cutting explanations are abundant, so the proposed count does not validly measure suppression of the phenomenon the problem is about.","target":"e7204e538e151c7765713c023ca433713f087b2438739e047f899faf67e0b555"}]}